{ His king is attacking your rook, and your own pawn needs a friend. One square does both. }
1... Re4 { Behind your own pawn, and safe. Now the pawn can never be blocked. [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge3e1] } (1... e2 { The pawn is in a hurry and runs straight into a rook. } 2. Rxe2+ Kd7 3. Kxb4 { Your pawn went, and now your rook too. Lost. }) (1... Ra4+ { A check with a hanging rook. } 2. Kxa4 { The rook is gone and the game with it. Lost. }) (1... Rb3+ { The same idea, the same problem. } 2. Kxb3 { Lost. }) (1... Rb2 { Guarding the pawn from the side, right next to his king. } 2. Kxb2 { Lost. }) (1... Kd6 { The king comes, but the rook was hanging. } 2. Kxb4 { Lost. }) 2. Re2 { He blocks in front of the pawn. It cannot move, so who can? } 2... Kd6 { The king marches over to help. Nothing else can. [%csl Gd6] } (2... Ke6 { Also good. Any step towards the pawn does the job. } 3. Kb2 Ke5 { Still winning. The king arrives just the same. }) (2... Kf6 { Also fine, and only one square slower. } 3. Kb2 Ke5 { You are still winning. }) 3. Kb2 { His king runs back to help. Keep coming. } 3... Ke5 { Straight at the pawn. Your king gets there first. [%csl Ge5] } (3... Kd5 { Also towards the pawn, but one row too low. } 4. Kc3 { His king slips in front and the win is gone. }) (3... Rd4 { The rook wanders off and leaves your pawn alone. } 4. Rxe3 { It is simply eaten. Drawn. }) 0-1


