{ A new word: cutting off. Build a wall with your rook and his king can never cross it. [%cal Yg2b2] }
1. Rb2 { The wall is up on the b column. His king is locked on the wrong side. [%csl Gb2][%cal Gb2b8] } (1. d6 { The pawn is in a hurry, and now the checks begin. } 1... Ra3+ 2. Ke2 Ra2+ { They never stop. Drawn. }) (1. Rg7+ { A check that chases him nowhere useful. } 1... Ka6 2. d6 Ra1 { He gets behind the pawn and holds. Drawn. }) (1. Ra2 { A trade offer he is happy to take. } 1... Rxa2 { His rook is left and yours is not. You are lost. }) (1. Rg4 { The rook steps into the open. } 1... Rxg4 { You are lost. }) (1. Kc3 { You walk the king up, but the checks come first. } 1... Ra3+ 2. Kb2 Rd3 { He gets in front of the pawn. Drawn. }) 1... Rf4 { He shuffles about behind the wall. Now walk your king up. [%cal Yd3e3] } 2. Ke3 { The king climbs, the wall holds, and the pawn follows. [%csl Ge3] } (2. Kc3 { Also winning. The king can climb on either side. } 2... Rf3+ 3. Kc4 Rf4+ { You are still winning. }) (2. Rb1 { Also fine. The wall is a column, not a square. } 2... Rf3+ { The checks come, but the wall still holds. }) (2. Rb5 { Also winning. The wall can slide up the column. } 2... Ka6 { You are still winning. }) (2. d6 { Too soon. The pawn needs the king, not a head start. } 2... Rf6 3. d7 Rd6+ { He wins the pawn. Drawn. }) (2. Rb7+ { A check that gives the rook away. } 2... Kxb7 { You are lost. }) (2. Ra2+ { You give up the wall for one check. } 2... Kb6 3. d6 Rf1 { He is back in the game. Drawn. }) 1-0


